+# Decision: Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per weekend event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision; browsers display a revisioned view of that board. How committed revisions reach up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers determines the responsibilities of three components designed together: the change store, the delivery service, and browser sync. Delivery is not an isolated transport choice — it fixes what each component must own.
+
+This decision rests on the architecture recorded in *Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event*: a committed edit carries exactly one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route, and offline editing is out of scope. Several standing constraints apply to every delivery approach and are background, not points of comparison: committed changes must appear within 15 seconds under normal connectivity; the system must sustain 800 browsers and opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second; connectivity failures must be visible rather than mistaken for an unchanged board; and both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted. All three options below satisfy these constraints. The decision turns on how the options trade propagation speed, independence from a realtime vendor, recurring cost, and the operational work the project takes on.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The weighted criteria below discriminate between the options. Importance uses **major / moderate / minor**. Where a criterion's importance derives from a tenet, the tenet is named. Every baseline constraint named in Context is respected by all three options, so none of them is a gate that eliminates an option from contention.
+
+- **Propagation latency** — moderate. How far below the 15-second target a committed change reaches browsers, including during opening-time bursts. Driven by the capability need for fast board updates.
+- **Independence from a realtime vendor** — major. How strongly the board's update path is tied to a third-party provider and its subscription model. The tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* makes this a weighed preference rather than a hard gate: a managed service is allowed, so coupling is a cost to weigh, not a prohibition.
+- **Recurring operating cost** — moderate. Fees that scale with connected browsers or message volume, as distinct from one-time implementation work.
+- **Operational and design work owned by the project** — moderate. Connection capacity, reconnect handling, replayable revisions, reliable publication, and browser catch-up that the project must build and run itself rather than delegate. The tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* sets the bar this work must meet: a reconnect must surface a gap, not silently present a stale board as current.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers periodically request the current board over HTTPS; the delivery service stays a stateless request/response endpoint and the change store serves cursor queries (`since` the last seen revision). No persistent connection is held. This is the simplest option to operate: there are no sessions to manage and no connection capacity to provision, and one browser's request has no effect on another's.
+
+**Demonstration.** Each browser issues `GET /events/{id}/board?since=<revision>` every five seconds and renders the returned revision; a failed request is an explicit error the UI can show.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Propagation latency: **weak**. A committed change waits up to five seconds for the next poll, and every browser re-requests the board even when nothing changed.
+- Independence from a realtime vendor: **strong**. Delivery runs entirely on the project's own HTTPS endpoint.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong**. No third-party service is paid; cost is the project's own request serving, which is modest.
+- Operational and design work owned by the project: **strong**. Stateless request/response needs cursor queries and a browser polling loop, but no session management, replay log, publication outbox, or reconnect/catch-up logic.
+
+**Summary.** Simplest to run, at the price of steady repeated reads and multi-second lag.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A managed realtime provider carries committed revisions to browsers over its own subscription model. The delivery service wraps the provider, the change store reconciles its authoritative revisions with provider publication, and browsers subscribe through the provider's SDK. The provider operates connection capacity, fan-out, and reconnect replays. This is the quickest route to low-latency updates with the least connection operations work for the project.
+
+**Demonstration.** The delivery service publishes `{revision, stallId, status}` to a provider channel; browsers receive it through the provider's subscription client.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Propagation latency: **strong**. Provider push is sub-second and scales with the provider's fan-out.
+- Independence from a realtime vendor: **weak**. The update path, browser subscription model, and credentials are owned by the provider; moving off it is a migration.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak**. The provider bills by connection or message volume, which grows with the 800-browser peak.
+- Operational and design work owned by the project: **strong**. Connection capacity and reconnect replays are delegated; the project still owns publication reconciliation and provider credentials.
+
+**Summary.** Low-latency board with the least connection ops, in exchange for vendor coupling and recurring fees.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The delivery service holds a WebSocket session for each connected browser and pushes every newly committed event revision to it as soon as it is published. The change store retains replayable revisions and a reliable publication outbox so no committed edit is lost between publication and delivery; delivery manages sessions and connection capacity; browsers implement reconnect and catch-up from the last revision they saw. The project runs the whole path.
+
+**Demonstration.** On commit, the outbox publishes `{revision, stallId, status}` to all live sessions for the event; a browser that reconnects after a drop sends its last revision and receives the missed revisions in order.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Propagation latency: **strong**. A committed revision is pushed over an already-open connection, typically sub-second.
+- Independence from a realtime vendor: **strong**. The update path runs on project-operated infrastructure with no provider coupling or credentials.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong**. No per-connection or per-message vendor fee; cost is the project's own socket capacity.
+- Operational and design work owned by the project: **weak**. The project owns connection capacity, reconnect storms around opening time, replayable revisions, a reliable publication outbox, and browser catch-up. This is the largest owned-work burden of the three options.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, vendor-independent updates, in exchange for owning connection and reconnect operations end to end.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is the only option that is strong on propagation latency, independence from a realtime vendor, and recurring operating cost at once — the criteria that carry weight in favor of the chosen direction. Polling is weak on propagation latency; the hosted service is weak on both vendor independence (major) and recurring cost (moderate). Neither reaches the fast, vendor-independent board this project wants.
+
+The deciding judgment is on the one criterion where self-hosted WebSockets is weak: operational and design work owned by the project. That weakness is accepted rather than avoided. The project takes on connection capacity, reconnect handling, replayable revisions, a reliable publication outbox, and browser catch-up in exchange for fast updates that carry no realtime-vendor dependency and no recurring fee. A reader inclined to reverse this choice would be weighing owned operations above vendor independence and cost; those are legitimate priorities, which is why the tradeoff is recorded here rather than assumed.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet *Self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted*: because managed infrastructure is allowed, "no realtime vendor" is a preference to weigh rather than a constraint that eliminated the hosted option outright — the hosted service is rejected on coupling and fees, not forbidden. It also turns on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*: the accepted reconnect/catch-up work must surface a disconnected browser's gap rather than leave a stale board looking current.
+
+Committing to self-hosted WebSockets forecloses stateless request/response delivery and provider-managed fan-out. It obligates the three downstream nodes, which are sized around a single revision stream: the change store owns replayable revisions and a publication outbox, the delivery service owns session management and reliable publication, and browser sync owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up. Those nodes are designed together against these obligations.
