 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets.** Delivery pushes each committed event revision to connected browsers over project-operated WebSocket sessions. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets deliver fast updates without binding the board to a realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection capacity, reconnect handling, and reliable publication. This choice sizes the change store (replayable revisions and a publication outbox), delivery (session management), and browser sync (reconnect and catch-up). Full criteria, options, and selection: [Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets](decisions/websocket-delivery.md).
